{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The label must be inserted as a truly payoff-harmless initial public price. If tail profiles include the initial observation in an unexpected way, the hidden and labeled classes may not be exactly profile-matched.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","proof strategy"],"summary":"Formalize the solver's tail-profile no-go pair. Compare the q-scaled hidden-terminal two-world class with a labeled version where P_1 reveals the world before all payoff-relevant quote decisions, while the future price law in each world is unchanged. Prove the hidden class has minimax regret Omega(qD), the labeled class has O(1) or zero grid regret, and the per-world beta, Delta, and delta profiles are identical up to the harmless shift.","title":"Tail-profile no-go pair","why_promising":"It would directly show that no scalar criterion depending only on individual laws' delay or truncation profiles can characterize unknown-class learnability; observability/predictability must enter."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"This is measure-theoretic and could get bogged down in policy-model choices. The result should preserve expected reward vectors only, not pathwise behavior.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurability audit"],"summary":"Write the original-policy alignment theorem for finite grids. Define admissible private-randomized Borel public-price policies exactly, prove by seed induction that a_t = alpha_t(P_{1:t}, U_{1:t}), and derive law-independent public-prefix kernels q_{t,a}(h) preserving all L_theta reward coordinates.","title":"Borel policy alignment","why_promising":"It is the main missing step for upgrading the completed L-infinity no-gap theorem under payoff-weighted domination to the intended original policy class."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Mixture-prefix null sets and regular conditional versions can obscure the equality. The explorer should keep finite G and finite F throughout.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measure theory"],"summary":"Prove that the density-form support formula under reward-measure domination equals the conditional Psi expression: sum_t integral max_a sum_theta pi_theta f^theta_{t,a} d lambda_t equals sum_t E_{mixture} max_a a Pr(M_t>a | P_{1:t}). Track all null sets and identify the mixture payoff-weighted density for each grid action.","title":"Density form equals Psi","why_promising":"It connects the compactness/no-gap proof to the accepted Bayes-predictability notation, closing a precise gap in the infinite-class theorem package."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Past block-splicing failed because the fixed comparator collapses to the Bayes action or one reveal learns the global type. This direction may only produce weaker lower bounds.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound design","information theory","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try a limited-leakage lower bound for power-tail rates. Build many delayed terminal experiments whose terminal observations give only small KL or TV information about a global type, so uncertainty persists across many blocks. Compute the Bayes gap via Psi_G and optimize q, D, number of blocks, and observation separation under Delta(H) <= T H^{-alpha}.","title":"Limited-leakage tail lower rates","why_promising":"Matching the truncation upper rate is the largest open quantitative gap; noisy information rather than one-shot hidden terminal reveals is a plausible way around the known failures."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A master over truncation windows may pay too much delayed-feedback overhead, and comparator-gap terms for different H may not combine cleanly.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","upper bound proof"],"summary":"Develop an adaptive truncation algorithm competing with the best dyadic H in hindsight. Run bounded-delay grid learners for multiple H values and aggregate them with a delayed-feedback master, targeting min_H {sqrt(T H log K) + T/K + delta_T(H)} plus logarithmic or lower-order overhead.","title":"Adaptive truncation upper bound","why_promising":"It would strengthen the sufficient theorem and may clarify whether the current tail-based upper rate is an artifact of choosing H in advance."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A fully Borel counterexample may not exist under natural identifiability assumptions; the search could turn into proving positive decoding/no-gap results instead.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","descriptive set theory","proof strategy"],"summary":"Search for a natural Borel, nondominated, non-identifiable infinite-class gap where finite subclasses have small value but the full Borel-policy class has linear value. If none is found, prove a positive theorem for identifiable standard-Borel singular classes with measurable decoding and measurable grid argmax.","title":"Natural infinite-class gap","why_promising":"The current non-Borel label obstruction is useful but pathological. A natural Borel obstruction or a positive identifiability theorem would clarify the true scope of the no-gap assumptions."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Standard doubling may partially rescue anytime performance; the two-deadline obstruction must be made exact against the fixed-quote comparator at selected horizons.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Formalize or refute the known-law horizon-free obstruction. Use a deterministic known infinite sequence with super-growing two-deadline blocks, where early horizon favors quote near 1/2 and later horizon favors quote near 1, and prove every single anytime policy has linear regret on one deadline subsequence.","title":"Anytime known-law boundary","why_promising":"The oracle lemma is horizon-aware. Settling the anytime boundary would prevent overclaiming and may reveal another necessary ingredient besides tails."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"For action-dependent prices, the problem may need a new causal regret definition before any theorem is meaningful.","suggested_lenses":["model formalization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit the action-dependent price regime. Define whether comparator rewards are evaluated on the learner-induced path, comparator-induced path, or a structural causal model; then test whether the delayed full-information truncation reduction fails through a concrete two-action price-response example.","title":"Action-dependent market model","why_promising":"The accepted upper bounds require exogenous public prices. A precise obstruction or alternate model statement would sharply delimit the theorem's applicability."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
